.TH MACHO-EXPLORER 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
diff \- differences in structure and literal content between two Mach-O binaries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho-explorer
.B diff
.I binary-a
.I binary-b
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-\-max= n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reports what changed between two binaries, over structural facts and literal
content: which architectures are present, which header flags are set, which load
commands and sections exist, which symbols are defined, and which strings are
present.
.SH WHY NOT CMP
.B cmp
between two builds of one source reports that every byte from the load base onwards
differs, because PIE and ASLR move every address, a dependency bump moves a dylib's
version fields, and a rebuild moves any timestamp. "Everything changed" cannot
distinguish a rebuilt binary from a different program, and cannot tell a reviewer
that the one thing they cared about did not move.
.PP
None of the facts compared here move on a rebuild, and all of them change when the
program does.
.SH THREE LISTS
.TP
.B differences
changes to structure or content. The verdict is computed from these alone. A
literal string that appears or disappears is one of these, matched by text: it
does not move on a rebuild, so it is a change to the program rather than build
noise.
.TP
.B buildMetadata
UUIDs, and the presence of signing and provenance commands.
.TP
.B sizeChanges
section sizes, reported because they matter and counted separately.
.PP
Build metadata is reported rather than hidden, and kept out of the verdict. A tool
that called every rebuilt pair "changed" would be
.B cmp
with better manners; one that hid the UUID would be refusing an answer the caller
asked for.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
nothing changed but build metadata and sizes
.TP
.B 1
the structure or the content changed \- a negative answer, not a failure
.TP
.B 2
usage error
.TP
.B 3
a file could not be read
.PP
Two builds of one program exit 0 with
.B buildMetadata
full. That is the case this tool exists for, and failing it would be absurd.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
compare only this architecture
.TP
.BI \-\-max= n
cap on symbol and string names listed per direction (default 20). Counts are
always exact; a 19,000-symbol binary would otherwise produce a diff nobody reads
past the first line.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
macho-explorer diff v1.0/libthing.dylib v1.1/libthing.dylib
macho-explorer diff \-\-json a b | jq '.data.differences[].category' | sort | uniq -c
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fingerprint (1),
.BR audit (1),
.BR describe (1)